---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';
import { getPracticeURL, withPracticeURL } from '../../lib/practice';
import axeDialogName from '../../samples/axe-dialog-name.txt?raw';
import axeHeadingOrder from '../../samples/axe-heading-order.txt?raw';
import checkBroken from '../../samples/check-broken-rules.txt?raw';
import mcpConfig from '../../samples/mcp-config.json.txt?raw';
import treeButton from '../../samples/tree-button.txt?raw';
import wcagStatus from '../../samples/wcag-413.txt?raw';

const practiceURL = getPracticeURL(Astro);
const fixedURL = `${practiceURL}fixed/`;
const toc = [
   { id: 'page', label: 'The two pages' },
   { id: 'scan', label: 'Problems 1 to 3: the scan' },
   { id: 'rules', label: 'Problems 4 and 5: rules you name' },
   { id: 'keyboard', label: 'Problems 6 and 7: the keyboard' },
   { id: 'dialog', label: 'Problem 8: opening the dialog' },
   { id: 'label', label: 'Problem 9: a pass that is not one' },
   { id: 'status', label: 'Problem 10: the confirmation' },
   { id: 'agent', label: 'The same run from an agent' },
   { id: 'fixes', label: 'The ten fixes' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Practice on a broken page"
   summary="<p>The practice page has ten accessibility problems. A second copy has all ten fixed. Run each command below against both and compare. A scan finds three of the problems. The other seven take a named rule, the keyboard, or a screen reader.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="page">The two pages</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><a href="/demo/team">The broken page</a></dt>
         <dd><code>{practiceURL}</code></dd>
         <dt><a href="/demo/team/fixed">The fixed page</a></dt>
         <dd><code>{fixedURL}</code></dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         The page is a team settings screen. A table lists three members. Each row has an
         avatar, a role, a status, a last-active date, and an icon button that removes the
         member. Above the table are a filter field, three status filter chips, and an
         <strong>Invite member</strong> button that opens a dialog. Submitting the dialog
         shows a confirmation above the table.
      </p>
      <p>
         Every difference between the two pages is one of the ten fixes. To switch pages in a
         screen reader session, run <code>a1 sr start</code> again with the other address. It
         replaces the session.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="scan">Problems 1 to 3: the scan</h2>
      <p>Start with <code>a1 check</code> on any page you have not tested before.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 check {practiceURL}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(checkBroken, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The report counts failing elements. Three rules fail on ten elements, and those
         three rules are the first three problems.
      </p>
      <ol>
         <li>The three avatar images have no <code>alt</code> attribute.</li>
         <li>The three remove buttons hold an icon and no text.</li>
         <li>
            The three last-active dates and the Pending status are gray on white at about
            2.3:1. The requirement is 4.5:1.
         </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
         The report's description of the page points to the next problem: the page has headings
         at levels 1 and 3, and nothing at level 2.
      </p>
      <p>
         On the fixed page the same command prints
         <code>✓ Nothing failed the automated checks.</code> It exits 4 on the broken page and
         0 on the fixed one, so a script can use it. See
         <a href="/guides/scripting">script the CLI</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="rules">Problems 4 and 5: rules you name</h2>
      <p>
         A default scan runs the axe rules that map to a WCAG criterion. axe has more rules
         that it tags as best practice. Run one of those by name with
         <code>a1 axe --rule</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe {practiceURL} --rule heading-order</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(axeHeadingOrder, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Problem 4: the Members heading is an <code>h3</code> directly under the page's
         <code>h1</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         Problem 5 is inside the dialog, and a scan sees only what is on the page when it
         runs. The practice page opens its dialog when the URL ends in <code>#invite</code>.
         On your own pages, <code>--click</code> does the same job: it clicks an element
         before the scan.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe '{practiceURL}#invite' --rule aria-dialog-name</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(axeDialogName, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The dialog has a heading and no accessible name. <code>a1 tree</code> prints the role
         and name of what a screen reader can reach, and shows the difference in one line
         each.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 tree '{practiceURL}#invite' --role dialog
a1 tree '{fixedURL}#invite' --role dialog</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{'- dialog\n\n- dialog "Invite member"'}</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="keyboard">Problems 6 and 7: the keyboard</h2>
      <p>List the buttons the accessibility tree reports.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 tree {practiceURL} --role button</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{treeButton.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The three nameless buttons are problem 2 again. All, Active, and Pending look fine
         here, and they are not. The tree reports a role. It cannot tell you whether a
         keyboard reaches the element. Start a session, then run the second line eight times.
         Each run presses Tab once and prints what the screen reader announced.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual {practiceURL}
a1 sr press Tab --phrase</code></pre>
      <div class="table-wrap">
         <table>
            <thead>
               <tr><th scope="col">Press</th><th scope="col">Broken page</th><th scope="col">Fixed page</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
               <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>link, General</td><td>link, General</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>link, Billing</td><td>link, Billing</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>link, Team, current page</td><td>link, Team, current page</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>searchbox, placeholder Filter members</td><td>searchbox, Filter members, placeholder Filter members</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">5</th><td>button, Invite member</td><td>button, All, pressed</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">6</th><td>button</td><td>button, Active, not pressed</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">7</th><td>button</td><td>button, Pending, not pressed</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">8</th><td>button</td><td>button, Invite member</td></tr>
            </tbody>
         </table>
      </div>
      <p>
         Problem 6: on the broken page, Tab goes from the filter field straight to Invite
         member. The chips are <code>&lt;div role="button"&gt;</code> with no
         <code>tabindex</code>, so a keyboard user cannot filter the table.
      </p>
      <p>
         Problem 7: on the fixed page the chips are real buttons, and each one says
         <code>pressed</code> or <code>not pressed</code>. On the broken page the dark
         background is the only sign of which filter is on.
      </p>
      <p>
         axe has a rule in this area, <code>focus-order-semantics</code>. It reports nothing
         here, because it checks elements that are in the tab order and these are not.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="dialog">Problem 8: opening the dialog</h2>
      <p>Move to the Invite member button, press it, and read where the cursor is.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr goto --role button --name "Invite member"
a1 sr activate
a1 sr read --phrase</code></pre>
      <div class="table-wrap">
         <table>
            <thead>
               <tr><th scope="col">Page</th><th scope="col">After the button is pressed</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
               <tr><th scope="row">Broken</th><td>button, Invite member</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">Fixed</th><td>textbox, Email address, required</td></tr>
            </tbody>
         </table>
      </div>
      <p>
         The broken page shows a <code>div</code> and leaves focus on the button. A screen
         reader user hears nothing. Tab from there walks the table behind the dialog, and
         Escape closes nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
         The fixed page calls <code>showModal()</code> on a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>. The
         browser then moves focus into the dialog, keeps Tab inside it, closes it on Escape,
         and returns focus to the button. No axe rule reports any of this. The criteria are
         2.4.3 Focus Order and 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap, and <code>a1 check</code> listed both
         under <strong>Check by hand</strong>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="label">Problem 9: a pass that is not one</h2>
      <p>
         Look at press 4 in the table above. The broken filter field is
         <code>&lt;input type="search" placeholder="Filter members"&gt;</code> with no label.
         axe accepts a placeholder as a name, so its <code>label</code> rule passes, and
         <code>a1 check --json</code> records criterion 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions with
         <code>axeVerdict: "pass"</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         The screen reader announces <code>searchbox, placeholder Filter members</code>: a
         field with a hint and no name. The hint disappears when the user types. The fix is
         a visible <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="status">Problem 10: the confirmation</h2>
      <p>
         With the dialog open, fill in the form and send it. The checkpoint marks the
         transcript so that <code>sr expect</code> reads only what came after it. The first
         line moves to the email field. The broken page needs it, because focus is not in the
         dialog.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr goto --role textbox --name "Email address"
a1 sr type "sam@northwind.test"
a1 sr checkpoint invite
a1 sr goto --role button --name "Send invitation"
a1 sr activate
a1 sr expect "Invitation sent" --since invite</code></pre>
      <div class="table-wrap">
         <table>
            <thead>
               <tr><th scope="col">Page</th><th scope="col">Matched</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
               <tr><th scope="row">Broken</th><td>nothing</td><td>fail, exit 4</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">Fixed</th><td>polite: Invitation sent to sam@northwind.test.</td><td>pass, exit 0</td></tr>
            </tbody>
         </table>
      </div>
      <p>
         Both pages show the same green confirmation. The fixed page puts it in a
         <code>role="status"</code> element that is on the page before the form is sent, so
         the screen reader announces it without moving focus. The broken page adds a plain
         <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, and a screen reader has no reason to read it.
      </p>
      <p>
         This is criterion 4.1.3 Status Messages. <code>a1 wcag</code> prints any criterion
         with how to test it and what to do when it fails.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 4.1.3</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{wcagStatus.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>Stop the session when you finish.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="agent">The same run from an agent</h2>
      <p>
         Each MCP tool calls the same function as the matching command, so an agent gets the
         same results. Add the server to your agent's MCP configuration.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{mcpConfig.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>{`Check ${practiceURL} with a11ied. Report every axe violation. Then test three things axe cannot: whether Tab reaches the status filter chips, whether opening the Invite member dialog moves focus into it, and whether sending the invite announces a confirmation. Compare each answer with ${fixedURL}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent calls <code>check</code>, then <code>run_axe</code> with
         <code>ruleIds</code>, <code>tree</code>, <code>sr_session</code>,
         <code>sr_action</code>, and <code>sr_expect</code>. See
         <a href="/guides/agents">use with an AI agent</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="fixes">The ten fixes</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
         <table>
            <thead>
               <tr><th scope="col">#</th><th scope="col">Found by</th><th scope="col">Fix</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
               <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td><code>image-alt</code></td><td>Avatars get <code>alt=""</code>. The member's name is already in the cell, so the image is decoration.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td><code>button-name</code></td><td>Each remove button gets <code>aria-label="Remove Dana Whitfield"</code>, and its icon gets <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td><code>color-contrast</code></td><td>The dates and the Pending status use darker text: 8.84:1 and 7.35:1.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td><code>heading-order</code>, by name</td><td>The Members heading becomes an <code>h2</code>.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">5</th><td><code>aria-dialog-name</code>, by name</td><td>The dialog gets <code>aria-labelledby</code>, pointing at the heading it already had.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">6</th><td>Tab</td><td>The filter chips become <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> elements. Tab reaches them, and Enter and Space work.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">7</th><td>Screen reader</td><td>The chips get <code>aria-pressed</code>.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">8</th><td>Screen reader</td><td>The dialog becomes a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> opened with <code>showModal()</code>.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">9</th><td>Screen reader</td><td>The filter field gets a visible <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">10</th><td>Screen reader</td><td>The confirmation goes in a <code>role="status"</code> element that is on the page from the start.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
         </table>
      </div>
      <p>
         Now run the same commands on your own page: <code>a1 check</code> first,
         <code>a1 tree</code> for names and roles, then <code>a1 sr</code> for what neither
         can report.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
